From 66db6c88039a7a1f227f05acc354d89fec86b9b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 19:40:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] generic-parallel: location of the arrays of the rotations,
 pending objects not assignable, statuses of the material properties

- The pending computations and evaluations can be moved but not assigned:
  with different memory resources, the assignment copied the statistics
  while the device could still write them.
- The rotations of a batch, with or without execution policy, take the
  location of their arrays (HOST by default), checked against the memory
  space of the behaviour, as the evaluation of a material property. The
  rotations without policy are performed on the host for the behaviours of
  the generic interface, which refuse arrays located on a device.
- buildRotationMatrices builds once the rotation matrices described by
  material axes, with a given memory resource.
- allocatePostProcessingVariables refuses a behaviour which can't access
  the memory of the host, and has an overload taking a memory resource. A
  null memory resource denotes the default resource everywhere, including
  for the asynchronous policies.
- The conversions of the finite strain stress and tangent operator of a
  material data manager check that its arrays are accessible by the host.
- A material property generated by the generic-parallel interface only
  returns the statuses 1, 0, -1 and -4: any other status is reported by an
  exception, at the submission of an asynchronous evaluation.
- Documentation: location of the values given by std::span to a material
  data manager and of the arrays given to the batch functions, statuses of
  the generic-parallel material properties, current limitations.

Tests: failures at some points on the GPU (index of the first failure,
synchronous, asynchronous, range), result which is not finite (host and
CUDA), refused locations, rotation matrices built once, rotation without
policy on the GPU, in place rotation, move semantics of the pending
objects.
